---
// The first class token below is load-bearing: below the md breakpoint the
// intro card's portrait and its scrim are both painted at a negative
// z-index, and this stacking context is what keeps them above this card's
// background instead of behind the page; the old perspective utility used to
// provide it accidentally.
// NB: UnoCSS scans .astro frontmatter, so never name a utility here that the
// template does not actually use — it emits a real CSS rule for a class
// nothing has. That is how the old perspective utility survived as dead CSS.
//
// The card has no hover state on purpose. All eight of them are plain
// containers: nothing about a card responds to a click, and the accent-coloured
// edge it used to take on under the pointer advertised an affordance that does
// not exist. The controls inside a card are the interactive elements and they
// keep theirs. The colour transition below stays — it carries the theme
// crossfade, not a pointer state.
//
// Wording matters here for the reason given above: an earlier draft of this
// paragraph used the ordinary English verb for "get bigger", which happens to
// be a flex utility, and UnoCSS emitted a rule for it that no element wore.
//
// WHY A CARD SIZES TO ITS CONTENT AND NEVER TO ITS GRID AREA.
//
// At the large breakpoint the page's rows have a set height, so a card's area is
// taller than its content, and a grid item stretches to fill its area by default.
// The difference showed up as an empty band under the last line a reader can see.
// Measured at 1440x900 from that last line down to the card's INNER edge — its
// content box, so the 24px of padding every card has below that is not counted:
// running goal 63px, cycling goal 63px, NCS role 111px, HeyMax role 43px,
// about-me 24px. Against the outer padding edge each figure is 24px larger.
//
// Those bands cannot be pooled and spent somewhere useful, because they sit in
// different columns — which is also why re-spanning does not remove them. Take the
// right-hand column, the one the report was about. Its three cards want 206, 206
// and 170px; a row there costs 92px including its gap, so each of them needs three
// rows, and that is nine rows for the eight the column has. It is over-subscribed
// already, which is why Now ends 2px into its own bottom padding and the footer
// 5px. Cutting a goal card to two rows gives it 168px and pushes its last line 29px
// past that same inner edge — of which 5px falls outside the clip edge itself, at
// the padding edge, and is the ink actually lost. Both figures are measured, not
// predicted; quote 29px only against the content box, never as content loss.
//
// Reclaiming the two empty rows that the role cards' six-row spans create does not
// help either, it hurts: those rows' 32px of gaps go back into the eight real rows,
// every row grows to 80px, and the goal band gets WORSE, 63px to 75px.
//
// The 92px is the reference viewport's, not a constant, and not one of a short list
// either. Every figure in this comment is also AT THE DEFAULT 16px ROOT: the clamp
// is written in rem now (index.astro), so all of it scales with the reader's text and
// none of these numbers is a fixture of the layout.
// `main` takes the viewport height, capped at 800px over a 736px floor, and
// its computed row template is ten tracks — the eight explicit ones plus two implicit
// tracks at zero height — so nine gaps of 16px are spent and a row is
// (clamp(736, viewport, 800) - 48 of padding - 144 of gaps) / 8. That is a continuum
// from 68px at the floor to 76px from a 800px viewport up, 72px at 768px tall, so a
// row costs 84px to 92px including its gap. The argument holds across the whole
// range, not just at those three points: 206, 206 and 170px of content each need
// three rows at EVERY integer row height in it, nine against the eight the column
// has. The arithmetic still has to be re-run, not quoted, if these spans change.
//
// So the card stops stretching instead. It takes its content's height and sits at
// the top of its area, which leaves the body directly under the heading where it
// belongs. The freed height does not become a gap between cards: the right-hand
// column is wrapped in its own stack in index.astro precisely so those three sit
// at the page gap and the leftover collects once, beneath the last of them. The
// cost, chosen by the maintainer over leaving the band alone and over splitting it
// above and below the body, is that a column's cards no longer end on a shared
// bottom edge — the unused height pools at the foot of each column instead.
//
// The block-size cap is the other half and it is not decoration, but be exact about
// what it can hold. A percentage cap resolves against the PARENT's height, and the
// parent is the grid area only for the five cards that are direct grid items; for the
// three inside the right-hand stack it is the stack's own height — 720px at 1440x900,
// over 500px more than any one of them asks for — so the cap cannot bind there at all,
// and what keeps those three inside their column is the stack, not the cap. Either
// way the percentage needs a parent height that is already decided, which is true
// only where the rows have a set height, so the cap clamps at the large breakpoint
// and does nothing at the smaller ones, where rows already size to their content.
//
// Where it does bind, measured: at the reference viewport exactly one card's content
// exceeds its area — the footer's, by 6px — and with the cap taken off the footer
// grows 76px to 82px, which is 6px past its own row and past the grid's content box,
// into main's bottom padding. At the 736px floor it holds two more, about-me and the
// HeyMax role card, whose content outgrows their shorter areas there.
//
// Two cards keep the height they ship with today exactly: the intro card at 352px,
// which is exempt anyway (below), and the footer at 76px. Now does not — it goes
// 168px to 170px, and that is the point rather than a side effect, because the 2px it
// used to spill past its own padding edge is now inside it. The five roomy cards
// shrink.
//
// Below 1024px there is nothing to fix. Rows there size to their content, so no
// card is stretched and no band exists — except one: between 768px and 1023px the
// NCS role card shares a six-row span with the taller HeyMax card and was pulled
// to 332px for 254px of content. The same two tokens fix that instance too.
//
// ONE CARD OPTS OUT, and it is the only card that had no band to begin with: the
// intro card, whose figure is 0px. It is measured differently from the five above,
// because it has to be — those are distances from the last TEXT line a reader sees,
// and this card has no text line below its row of controls, so its figure is the
// lowest painted box instead: at 1440x900 that box ends at 401px and so does the
// content box, in both revisions. (A last-line instrument reports 173px here, off the
// tagline, which is why no band figure for this card is comparable to the others'.)
//
// It is the exception for a mechanical reason, not a cosmetic one. Its inner row asks
// for its full height as a percentage, and a percentage needs a parent whose height
// is already decided. While the card stretched, the grid row decided it. A card sized
// to its own content has no decided height for that percentage to resolve against, so
// the card's two halves stack at their natural heights and its content asks for 355px
// inside a 318px padding box, while the cap still holds the box at its area. What
// then falls outside the clip edge is the BOTTOM ROW OF THREE CONTROLS — Strava,
// Telegram and the résumé link — by 6px at 1024x600, where the page sits on its 736px
// floor and the rows are at their shortest. Not the portrait: that renders at its
// 275px attribute height with the exemption or without it, and stays 3px inside the
// clip edge either way. Letting this one card go on stretching is a smaller change
// than rebuilding how the intro card distributes its own two halves, and it costs
// nothing, because a card with no spare height has nothing to hand back. Anything
// else added to this page should NOT take the exemption.
//
// THE HEADING OWNS THE SPACE BELOW IT, and it owns it here so that every card taking
// this component's `title` prop gets the same amount — five of the page's six card
// headings, measured 16px each. The sixth is the Now card's, which is written out in
// Now.astro rather than passed as `title` and reserves 0px; that card is deliberately
// outside this rule, so "every card heading" is not the claim.
//
// It did not hold before either: measured at 1440x900 from the heading's bottom to the
// top of the first thing under it, the goal cards had 16px — supplied by the progress
// bar's own top margin, which is why they looked right — while about-me and both role
// cards had 1px and read as cramped. The margin moved onto the heading and came off
// the bar; keeping both would double the goal cards' gap to 32px.
//
// The space has no breakpoint variant, on purpose — a card heading reads as cramped at
// every width — so it is spent below the large breakpoint too, where nothing is
// clipped and the page simply gets taller. Measured base-to-branch: about-me, HeyMax
// and NCS gain 16px each, and the page grows 35px at 1023x900, 42px at 768x900 and
// 48px at 320x568. One case worth knowing rather than rediscovering: at 834x1112,
// 10.5-inch tablet portrait, the page went from exactly fitting the viewport (1112px)
// to 1128px, so it now scrolls there where it did not before.
//
// That space is a FONT-RELATIVE length, and it spent one revision as an absolute
// one. Both spellings were right about their own revision, which is why the reason
// belongs here and not just the unit.
//
// It was pinned to 16px because a card's height came from the page rows, those rows
// did not scale with the root font-size, and every card clips — so a gap that grew
// with the reader's text was ink deleted, and the relative spelling measurably lost
// content at a 17px root where the revision before it lost none. The gap was not the
// disease. The page's height budget was: `main` was clamped between two absolute
// lengths, so the grid could not get taller while the type inside it did.
//
// That is corrected at the source now (index.astro for the budget, uno.config.ts for the
// breakpoints), so the premise for pinning this is gone and the gap goes back to
// scaling with the type it holds off. Measured as ink past each card's clip edge,
// summed over the page at 1024x800: this revision loses 0 at a 17px root, 0 at 18,
// 0 at 20, 0 at 22 and 0 at 24, against 4.8 / 196 / 457 / 966 / 1596 before — and 0
// out to a 40px root across eight viewports from 320 to 2560 wide.
//
// Two things not to do with those numbers. They are ink past the CLIP edge summed
// over eight cards, which is not the quantity an earlier version of this comment
// quoted as "8 / 3.5 / 14 / 230 / 480": that series mixed viewports, naming the one
// where each figure first appeared, so it reads as a single sweep and is not one.
// And do not "fix" a future cramped reading by shrinking this gap: the cramped
// reading it corrects was reported.
interface Props {
    title?: string;
    titleIcon?: string;
    colSpan?: string;
    rowSpan?: string;
    /** Keep filling the grid area instead of sizing to content. See above — the intro card only. */
    fillsArea?: boolean;
}

const {title, titleIcon, colSpan, rowSpan, fillsArea} = Astro.props;
---

<div
        data-card
        class={`isolate ${fillsArea ? "" : "self-start max-h-full"} overflow-hidden bg-[var(--card-background)] shadow-lg rounded-lg p-6 border border-[var(--card-border)] relative w-full transition-colors duration-300 ease-in-out col-span-1 ${colSpan || "md:col-span-2"} ${rowSpan || ""}`}
>
    {title && <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mt-0 mb-4">{titleIcon && <span class={titleIcon} aria-hidden="true"></span>} {title}</h2>}
    <slot/>
</div>
